  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">KAM REACH</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">KAM REACH</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The REACH project focused on potato and rice cultivation. Both crops are relevant for smallholder farmers (SHF) and have potential to be grown as cash crop. The interest of farmers in these crops is shown by the fact that farmers do rent land to cultivate and some are willing to invest in expensive seed (potato).   The project reports an increase of income from potatoes and rice. Although this is likely seen the type of interventions, the attribution could not be made specific due to weather challenges in the baseline year. The yields for both crops, although improved, did not reach the targets set, although farmers adopt in general (90%) good agricultural practices (GAP) and climate smart practices (CSA). This implies that non-technical reasons, like risk-mitigation and access to finance, may keep yields low.  For GAP and CSA practices, the project focused its activities on the potato and rice plots specifically. It also is important for future activities, to consider the broader farming system when addressing environmental issues and promoting sustainable land-use.  Reach achieved promising improvements in the potato-seed sector. Both technical perspective (tissue-culture) and propagation of improved material. At the same time it is noticed that SHF do not yet have good access (imported seeds) or can not afford the quality declared seed locally propagated. The challenge for the future is to further develop this sub-sector in order that smallholder farmers can profit from the improvements.   The project was successful involving private sector players in the two value chains. Challenges that benefit from private sector actors involvement are seed quality and for potato and processing for rice and potato.The collaboration with commercial farmers and market actors led to some improvements in the market chains only after capital investment by the project. This collaboration may require further reflection, also on how the majority of SHF can be better included in the market system development approach. So far this group did not benefit from the activities as anticipated.</narrative>
  </description>
